# HTML: Inline-Level Tags vs Block-Level Tags

## 📌 Introduction

In HTML, elements are commonly categorized as **Block-Level Elements** and **Inline-Level Elements** based on how they behave on a webpage.

Understanding the difference helps us structure content and control its layout.

## 1. Block-Level Elements

**Definition:** Block-level elements typically start on a new line and occupy the full available width of their parent container by default.

### Examples

* `<h1>` to `<h6>` — Heading tags
* `<p>` — Paragraph tag
* `<ul>` — Unordered list
* `<ol>` — Ordered list
* `<li>` — List item
* `<div>` — Division or container
* `<pre>` — Preformatted text
* `<blockquote>` — Block quotation

### Example Code

```html
<h1>Hello World</h1>
<p>This is a paragraph.</p>
<div>This is a block-level element.</div>
```

### Output Behavior

Each element typically starts on a new line.

## 2. Inline-Level Elements

**Definition:** Inline elements do not typically start on a new line. They occupy only the space required by their content and flow alongside other inline content.

### Examples

* `<a>` — Anchor tag
* `<b>` — Bold text
* `<strong>` — Important text
* `<i>` — Italic text
* `<em>` — Emphasized text
* `<span>` — Inline container
* `<sub>` — Subscript text
* `<sup>` — Superscript text
* `<img>` — Image
* `<u>` — Underlined text

### Example Code

```html
<p>
    This is <b>bold</b> text.
    This is <i>italic</i> text.
    Visit <a href="https://example.com">Example</a>.
</p>
```

### Output Behavior

Inline elements generally appear within the same line as the surrounding text.

## 3. Difference Between Block-Level and Inline-Level Elements

| Feature    | Block-Level Elements                  | Inline-Level Elements                      |
| ---------- | ------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------ |
| New Line   | Typically start on a new line         | Typically stay on the same line            |
| Width      | Occupy the available width by default | Occupy the space required by their content |
| Layout     | Usually stack vertically              | Usually flow horizontally with text        |
| Examples   | `<div>`, `<p>`, `<h1>`                | `<span>`, `<a>`, `<b>`                     |
| Common Use | Structuring webpage content           | Styling or marking text within content     |

## 4. Important Points to Remember

1. Block-level elements typically begin on a new line.
2. Inline elements generally remain within the current line.
3. CSS can change an element's display behavior using properties such as `display: block`, `display: inline`, and `display: inline-block`.
4. An inline element generally cannot contain a block-level element under traditional HTML content rules. However, HTML validity depends on the specific elements and their content models.
5. Modern HTML specifications classify elements according to their content models rather than using the old block-level and inline-level categories. These terms are still useful for learning their default layout behavior.

## 5. Practice Example

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Block vs Inline Elements</title>
</head>
<body>

    <!-- Block-level elements -->
    <h1>Learning HTML</h1>
    <p>This is a paragraph.</p>
    <div>This is a div element.</div>

    <!-- Inline elements -->
    <p>
        This is <b>bold</b> text,
        <i>italic</i> text, and
        <a href="https://example.com">a link</a>.
    </p>

</body>
</html>
```

## 🎯 What I Learned

* The difference between block-level and inline-level elements.
* Common examples of both types of elements.
* How block and inline elements behave on a webpage.
* How CSS can change the default display behavior of HTML elements.

